    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Koira',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Koira (Finnish for 'Dog') is a traditional Finnish card game for three to eight players. It is an avoidance game belonging to the 'beating' family (like Skitgubbe). The game is famous for its social element: the loser is called the 'Dog' and may be jokingly required to bark. It is played in two distinct phases: a collection phase where card count is expanded, and an avoidance phase where players try to empty their hands.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Originates from 19th-century Finnish folk traditions, closely related to the Swedish game Skitgubbe and the Norwegian Mattis. It was historically a common game among forestry workers and farming families.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '3 players is the classic competitive format.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck. Ace is high, 2 is low.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck. Ace is high, 2 is low.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Each player receives 3 cards. The rest form a stock pile.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Tie: take back and deal: When there is a tie for highest card in phase 1, the tied players take their cards back; the other cards in the trick are arranged in ascending order and dealt one at a time to the tied players as evenly as possible, counterclockwise from the last player who played a tied card.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Trump ends trick: In phase 2, a trump played on a non-trump immediately ends the trick; the player of the trump sets the cards aside and leads to a new trick.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Pick lowest card only: When picking up from a trick, only the lowest card is taken; if the lowest play is a sequence, only the bottom card of that sequence is taken. A sequence counts as in play until all its cards have been removed.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
